<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeclanum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aeclanum was the main town of the Hirpini in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, near modern Mirabella Eclano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> captured and sacked it in 89 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>, after which it became a Roman municipium on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a>. Its 5th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Julian of Eclanum, was a leading opponent of Augustine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeclanum.jpg" alt="Aeclanum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dэя-Бøяg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aeclanum_(Thermae-01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aeclanum, also spelled Aeculanum, lay about 25 kilometres east-south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>, at Passo di Mirabella. The town stood on a promontory defended to some extent by a steep slope on its south side down to the river Calore, while the north side lay open towards the crest of the ridge where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a> ran on through Lacus Ampsanctus to Aquilonia and Venusia. Two other routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-aemilia">Via Aemilia</a> in Hirpinis and the Via Aurelia Aeclanensis, branched off nearby towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Luceria</a> and Herdoniae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavation has shown a long history of pre-Roman settlement, although the town is never mentioned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> took it in 89 BCE he did so by setting fire to the wooden breastwork that defended it. The town recovered quickly and built new fortifications. The emperor Hadrian, who repaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> to this point, made it a colonia.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the 6th century it was annexed to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. In 663 Eastern Roman forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> captured and destroyed it, and it never recovered, shrinking to a hamlet called Quintodecimo, a name that recalled its distance of 15 Roman miles from Benevento.</p><p>The town was a Christian bishopric. Julian of Eclanum was consecrated by Pope Innocent I in about 417, refused to sign the condemnation of Pelagianism issued by Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>, and carried on a war of writings against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>. Quintodecimo is named as a suffragan see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> in 969 and 1058, and from 1059 the diocese was united with that of Frequentium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is now an archaeological park. Its remains include parts of the city walls, an aqueduct, baths and a macellum, and nearly 400 inscriptions have been found there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> lists Aeclanum as a titular see.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aeclanum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their centres included Maleventum (later Beneventum), <b>Aeclanum</b>, Compsa and Abellinum.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirpini">Hirpini</a></li></ul></section>
